            <ArticleTitle>Improvement of quality of life following transcatheter aortic valve implantation in the elderly: a multi-centre study based on the Polish national TAVI registry.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5603/KP.a2016.0164</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) is an effective therapeutic method for elderly high-risk patients with symptomatic severe aortic stenosis, not eligible for surgical treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the study was to evaluate short-term changes in elderly people's quality of life following TAVI, based on the POL-TAVI registry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and eighty-four octogenarians' and nonagenarians' samples from the POL-TAVI database were included in the study. The quality of life status was assessed with EQ-5D-3L questionnaire at baseline and after one-month follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean of patients' age was 84 (80-93) years. Logistic EuroSCORE mean was 19.83% (3.48-83.94%), and transaortic mean gradient was 54.99 (19.0-149.0) mm Hg. At baseline, up to 24.50% of patients declared severe problems in performing usual activities. 13.60% felt extreme pain or discomfort; 9.80% were unable to wash and dress by themselves; 8.20% were extremely anxious or depressed; and 7.10% of the patients in the study were confined to bed. After 30 days the percentage of patients declaring severe problems in each dimension did not exceed 4.00%. The comparison between quality of life data at baseline and after one-month follow-up showed a significant improvement in each of five dimensions: mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain/discomfort, and anxiety/depression (p &lt; 0.001). Patients' quality of life was increasing regardless of gender (female vs. male), the type of anaesthesia (general vs. local), and the type of procedure (TA vs. TF).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Short-term quality of life in the elderly subjects was significantly improved after one-month follow-up following TAVI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
